<h2>The situation</h2>
<p>A Singapore digital insurer sold travel, health and home policies online. Its policy administration, claims and payments ran on its own cloud estate across two availability zones, with tested failover. Everything else ran on one productivity suite: email, chat, video calls, shared documents, the identity provider staff used to sign in to every other system, and the AI assistant built into it. Customer notices, incident bridges and even the runbooks for failing over the core platform lived there.</p>
<p>MAS's business continuity management guidelines expect financial institutions to identify their critical business services and set, for each, a service recovery time objective: how long it can be disrupted before severe impact, with the recovery times of underlying functions and dependencies fitting inside it<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>. The insurer had done this for its platforms. It had not traced the dependencies through the productivity suite.</p>
<p><strong>Why now.</strong> On 12 November 2026, a long Microsoft 365 outage disrupted Outlook, Exchange Online and Teams for much of a working day; Microsoft attributed it to servers failing health checks and dropping out of service, with no evidence of an attack<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>. Staff across the industry found they could not email customers, join incident calls or open runbooks. The insurer's board asked a simple question: what would we have done?</p>

<h2>What had to be true</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Dependencies known</strong>: each critical service mapped to the SaaS it relies on, identity included.</li>
<li><strong>Targets met</strong>: workarounds that keep each service within its recovery target.</li>
<li><strong>Coordination possible</strong>: a way to run an incident without email, chat or shared documents.</li>
<li><strong>Customers reachable</strong>: notices that do not depend on the failed suite.</li>
<li><strong>Access preserved</strong>: administrators can reach critical systems if the identity provider is down.</li>
<li><strong>Early warning</strong>: the insurer knows about an outage before staff start complaining.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Architecture</h2>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-stack">
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Critical services</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Sell policies</span><span>Pay claims</span><span>Serve customers</span><span>Meet regulatory duties</span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Daily tools</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Productivity suite<em>email, chat, documents, AI</em></span><span class="hl">Identity provider<em>sign-in to everything</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Fallbacks</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">Out-of-band messaging<em>separate provider</em></span><span>Paging and SMS</span><span>Independent status page</span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Emergency access</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">Break-glass accounts<em>outside the main identity provider</em></span><span>Offline runbooks<em>and contact lists</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Detection</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Synthetic checks<em>from outside, several regions</em></span><span>Provider status feeds</span></div></div>
</div>
<figcaption>The fallbacks sit on different providers from the daily tools, so one outage cannot take out both.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>The first deliverable was a dependency map for each critical business service, built with the people who run them. It showed that the identity provider was the biggest single point of failure: if staff could not sign in, they could not reach the claims system, the cloud console or the paging tool, even though those were healthy.</p>

<h2>The data flow, end to end</h2>
<figure class="dgm">
<ol class="dgm-flow g3">
<li><b>Detect</b><span>Synthetic checks from several regions see the suite failing</span></li>
<li><b>Page</b><span>On-call staff are paged through a separate paging service</span></li>
<li class="hl"><b>Coordinate</b><span>The incident moves to the out-of-band messaging tool</span></li>
<li><b>Decide</b><span>Leaders choose workarounds per service, using offline runbooks</span></li>
<li class="hl"><b>Communicate</b><span>Customers and partners are told via the status page and SMS</span></li>
<li><b>Recover</b><span>Normal tools return; the incident record feeds the next exercise</span></li>
</ol>
<figcaption>Every step uses something that does not depend on the suite that failed.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure class="fig"><div class="im"><img src="/assets/img/singapore-insurer-saas-outage-continuity/synthetic-checks.webp" alt="Chart of httpcheck.duration over time for three HTTP endpoints, with a table showing minimum, maximum and average response times per URL" width="1400" height="763" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption>Synthetic HTTP checks give an independent view of an endpoint's health: the insurer pointed them at the suite's sign-in, mail and chat endpoints. <span class="credit">Source: <a href="https://opentelemetry.io/blog/2023/synthetic-testing/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OpenTelemetry blog, Carter Socha</a> · CC BY 4.0</span></figcaption></figure>
<p>Detection used the OpenTelemetry Collector's HTTP check receiver, run from small collectors in more than one region, to probe the suite's sign-in, mail and chat endpoints alongside the insurer's own services<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>. Results went to the observability platform the insurer already used, which did not depend on the suite.</p>

<h2>The hard part: identity and the fallback nobody uses</h2>
<p><strong>Break-glass without a back door.</strong> Emergency accounts that bypass the main identity provider are exactly what attackers want. The insurer kept a small number, each tied to a named role, protected with hardware security keys, stored with sealed procedures, monitored for any use and tested on a schedule. Any use outside an exercise raised an immediate alert to security.</p>
<p><strong>Keeping fallbacks alive.</strong> A fallback channel that nobody opens for months will not work when needed: accounts expire, numbers change, people forget. The insurer used the out-of-band messaging tool for real on-call handovers every week, synchronised its contact lists from the HR system daily, and printed a short card of first steps for on-call staff.</p>
<p><strong>Regulatory duties without email.</strong> Incident reporting to MAS and notices to customers normally went out by email. The runbook named an alternative route for each, and the compliance team rehearsed it, so a suite outage would not also become a reporting failure.</p>
<div class="callout"><p>Resilience plans usually cover your systems failing. The outage that hurts is often the supplier your plan quietly depends on: the one hosting the plan itself.</p></div>

<h2>How it ran</h2>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-lanes">
<div class="dgm-lane"><small>Technology</small><ol><li>Dependency maps</li><li class="hl">Synthetic checks and paging</li><li>Break-glass access</li></ol></div>
<div class="dgm-lane"><small>Operations</small><ol><li>Workarounds per service</li><li class="hl">Offline runbooks</li><li>Weekly use of fallbacks</li></ol></div>
<div class="dgm-lane"><small>Risk and compliance</small><ol><li>Recovery targets</li><li>Regulatory reporting routes</li><li class="hl">Exercise evidence</li></ol></div>
<div class="dgm-lane"><small>Customer teams</small><ol><li>Status page</li><li>SMS templates</li><li class="hl">Partner contacts</li></ol></div>
</div>
<figcaption>Technology built the alternatives; operations made them habits; risk turned them into evidence.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-phases">
<div><small>Phase 1</small><b>Map</b><ul><li>Critical services</li><li>SaaS dependencies</li><li>Identity paths</li></ul></div>
<div><small>Phase 2</small><b>Build</b><ul><li>Out-of-band channel</li><li>Break-glass access</li><li>Synthetic checks</li></ul></div>
<div><small>Phase 3</small><b>Document</b><ul><li>Offline runbooks</li><li>Customer templates</li><li>Reporting routes</li></ul></div>
<div><small>Phase 4</small><b>Exercise</b><ul><li>Tabletop</li><li>Live switch to fallbacks</li><li>Lessons into plans</li></ul></div>
</div>
<figcaption>Mapping changed the plan: identity, not email, turned out to be the critical dependency.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2>Key decisions and trade-offs</h2>
<table class="tbl">
<thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Alternative</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Fallbacks on different providers</td><td>Backup features of the same suite</td><td>One outage cannot take out both</td></tr>
<tr><td>Use fallbacks weekly</td><td>Keep them for emergencies only</td><td>Unused tools fail when needed</td></tr>
<tr><td>Small, monitored break-glass set</td><td>No emergency access, or many accounts</td><td>Access when needed without a standing back door</td></tr>
<tr><td>Independent synthetic checks</td><td>Rely on the provider's status page</td><td>Earlier warning and your own evidence</td></tr>
<tr><td>Offline runbooks and printed first steps</td><td>Runbooks only in shared documents</td><td>Available when the suite is not</td></tr>
<tr><td>Workarounds sized to recovery targets</td><td>Generic "wait for the provider"</td><td>Meets MAS expectations per service</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h2>What I'd do again, and differently</h2>
<p>Again: map dependencies service by service with the people who run them; it surfaced the identity dependency nobody had written down. Differently: include the AI assistant in the map from the start; some teams had begun relying on it for routine customer replies, and its outage was a gap in its own right. Related: <a href="/insights/tech-news-2026-11-13/">the 13 November tech news</a>, the <a href="/case-studies/ai-provider-resilience-cps-230-insurer/">AI provider resilience case study</a>, <a href="/insights/slos-error-budgets-small-teams/">SLOs and error budgets</a> and the <a href="/fractional-cto-singapore/">Singapore page</a>.</p>
<p>If a single SaaS outage would stop you coordinating an incident or reaching customers, see my <a href="/fractional-cto/">fractional CTO work</a>, or <a href="/#assess">analyse your challenge</a> and bring the result to a call.</p>
